1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,983
Wednesday, 19 September 2012, Swedish public television.

2
00:00:02,983 --> 00:00:08,152
The investigative programme Uppdrag granskning broadcasts an episode titled, plainly, "TeliaSonera in Uzbekistan".

3
00:00:08,152 --> 00:00:25,248
Its finding: Sweden's former telephone monopoly, still more than a third owned by the Swedish state, had paid roughly two point two billion kronor, some three hundred and twenty million dollars, to a Gibraltar shell company for the right to operate in Uzbekistan.

4
00:00:25,248 --> 00:00:28,429
The shell had no office and no staff.

5
00:00:28,429 --> 00:00:36,381
Its registered owner was a twenty-four-year-old who had run a fashion boutique in Tashkent for Gulnara Karimova, the president's daughter.

6
00:00:36,381 --> 00:00:41,550
To understand how a Swedish state company got there, go back to 2007.

7
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:46,027
That was the company's position on the night, and it is worth keeping.

8
00:00:46,027 --> 00:00:50,417
Not a denial that the money was paid: the payments were in the accounts.

9
00:00:50,417 --> 00:00:56,688
A statement that the company did not know where three hundred million dollars had gone once it left the building.

10
00:00:56,688 --> 00:01:01,392
Chief executive Lars Nyberg went further and staked his job on being cleared of bribery.

11
00:01:01,392 --> 00:01:03,587
He would be gone within five months.

12
00:01:03,587 --> 00:01:09,545
The Swedish state, which owned thirty-seven point three percent and nominated the chairman, said it had known nothing either.

13
00:01:09,545 --> 00:01:11,113
That is the recurring line.

14
00:01:11,513 --> 00:01:12,780
The company itself.

15
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:20,384
Telia was Televerket, the Swedish state telephone administration, turned into a company in 1993 and floated in 2000.

16
00:01:20,384 --> 00:01:30,100
In 2002 it merged with Sonera, Finland's equivalent, and inherited Sonera's holdings in the former Soviet south, through a Turkish-linked vehicle called Fintur.

17
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:44,041
In 2012 the Swedish state still held thirty-seven point three percent, Finland eleven point seven, and headquarters sat at Stureplan in central Stockholm, a short walk from the finance ministry that owned it.

18
00:01:44,041 --> 00:01:46,998
Eurasia was, by then, the growth engine.

19
00:01:47,398 --> 00:01:48,221
The geography.

20
00:01:48,221 --> 00:01:51,512
Stockholm and Helsinki, the two former state monopolies.

21
00:01:51,512 --> 00:01:59,741
Tashkent, capital of Uzbekistan, thirty million people, a largely unconnected market and one of the most repressive states on earth.

22
00:01:59,741 --> 00:02:03,032
Almaty, Baku, Tbilisi, Kathmandu: the other Eurasian markets.

23
00:02:03,032 --> 00:02:05,089
Gibraltar, where Takilant was registered.

24
00:02:05,089 --> 00:02:08,792
Geneva, where the money was banked and later frozen.

25
00:02:08,792 --> 00:02:24,426
And two places with no role in 2007 and every role afterwards: The Hague, because Telia held its Uzbek assets through Dutch companies, and New York and Washington, because payments cleared in dollars and Telia's shares traded there.

26
00:02:24,426 --> 00:02:28,951
Nothing on this map is in Sweden except the head office.

27
00:02:29,351 --> 00:02:30,199
The entry.

28
00:02:30,199 --> 00:02:41,219
In July 2007 TeliaSonera bought MCT Corp, an American-registered company that owned Coscom, an Uzbek operator with a few hundred thousand subscribers and no 3G frequencies.

29
00:02:41,219 --> 00:02:48,848
Frequencies in Uzbekistan were, in practice, dispensed by people close to Gulnara Karimova, through her intermediary Bekhzod Akhmedov.

30
00:02:48,848 --> 00:02:57,748
In December 2007 TeliaSonera signed with Takilant, a Gibraltar company owned on paper by Gayane Avakyan, twenty-four, of Karimova's fashion house.

31
00:02:57,748 --> 00:03:07,496
Takilant would take twenty-six percent of Coscom's holding company for thirty million dollars, with the right to sell it back at a profit.

32
00:03:07,496 --> 00:03:08,767
The frequencies arrived.

33
00:03:08,767 --> 00:03:13,853
Board papers that year, American investigators later found, noted the "anti-corruption perspective".

34
00:03:13,853 --> 00:03:15,549
The deal went ahead.

35
00:03:15,949 --> 00:03:21,040
What Takilant received, as later set out by the US Department of Justice.

36
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:26,522
Thirty million dollars in 2007 for the shares that came with the 3G frequencies.

37
00:03:26,522 --> 00:03:30,830
Nine point two million in 2008 for number series and codes.

38
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:40,620
Two hundred and twenty million in 2010, when TeliaSonera bought back the twenty-six percent it had sold three years earlier, at seven times the price.

39
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:50,019
Then sham consulting contracts, a fifteen million dollar debt of a Swiss company owned by Karimova, and two million in cash to her representative.

40
00:03:50,019 --> 00:03:53,152
Total: about three hundred and thirty-one million dollars.

41
00:03:53,152 --> 00:03:58,242
A further payment was under discussion in late 2012, until Swedish television intervened.

42
00:03:58,642 --> 00:03:59,418
The recipient.

43
00:03:59,418 --> 00:04:18,412
Gulnara Karimova was her father's ambassador in Madrid and Geneva, a jewellery and fashion designer, a pop singer under the name Googoosha, and, according to a 2005 American diplomatic cable released by WikiLeaks, viewed by Uzbeks as "a robber baron" and "the single most hated person in the country".

44
00:04:18,412 --> 00:04:23,063
She held no post in telecoms regulation and did not need one.

45
00:04:23,063 --> 00:04:29,265
Every foreign operator entering Uzbekistan dealt with her structure: TeliaSonera, Russia's MTS and the Dutch-registered VimpelCom.

46
00:04:29,265 --> 00:04:35,467
By the American reckoning she extracted around eight hundred and sixty-five million dollars from the three.

47
00:04:35,467 --> 00:04:41,282
TeliaSonera was the largest contributor, and the only one partly owned by a Nordic government.

48
00:04:41,682 --> 00:04:53,628
The reporters, Fredrik Laurin, Sven Bergman and Joachim Dyfvermark, had traced Takilant from Gibraltar to a Lombard Odier account in Geneva, where Swiss prosecutors had already opened a laundering case.

49
00:04:53,628 --> 00:05:03,981
A week after the broadcast the Swedish Prosecution Authority opened an inquiry into aggravated bribery, and in October a court froze Takilant funds held in Stockholm.

50
00:05:03,981 --> 00:05:09,555
Ucell, the Uzbek subsidiary, by then had nine million subscribers and was highly profitable.

51
00:05:09,555 --> 00:05:17,519
In Sweden the mood was closer to shock than outrage; the sum was unheard of in a Swedish bribery context.

52
00:05:17,519 --> 00:05:25,085
Sweden ranked fourth on Transparency International's index that year; Uzbekistan, a hundred and seventieth of a hundred and seventy-six.

53
00:05:25,485 --> 00:05:29,603
The unwinding took three years and most of the top table.

54
00:05:29,603 --> 00:05:37,838
The board commissioned the law firm Mannheimer Swartling; its report landed on 1 February 2013 and the chief executive left that morning.

55
00:05:37,838 --> 00:05:41,207
The chairman went in April, replaced by Marie Ehrling.

56
00:05:41,207 --> 00:05:51,314
A new chief executive, Johan Dennelind, arrived in September and ordered a review by Norton Rose Fulbright of every Eurasian deal; four executives were dismissed in November.

57
00:05:51,314 --> 00:06:02,544
In Tashkent, Karimova fell out with her father and was confined to her house in February 2014; a month later Telia disclosed that the Americans and the Dutch were investigating.

58
00:06:02,544 --> 00:06:09,657
In September 2015, three years almost to the day after the broadcast, it said it would leave Eurasia entirely.

59
00:06:10,057 --> 00:06:12,426
The lawyers' verdict, and note the construction.

60
00:06:12,426 --> 00:06:14,796
Not innocent, not guilty: unproven, both ways.

61
00:06:14,796 --> 00:06:26,982
The report said the company had failed to check who was behind Takilant, that its ethical guidelines had "certainly been violated", and that it had asked only whether payments were lawful, not whether they were wise.

62
00:06:26,982 --> 00:06:31,382
Nyberg said the review had cleared the company of bribery and resigned anyway.

63
00:06:31,382 --> 00:06:36,121
Chairman Anders Narvinger conceded it was "absolutely clear" the partner should have been vetted.

64
00:06:36,121 --> 00:06:41,876
Two months later he too was gone, at the request of the largest shareholder, the Swedish government.

65
00:06:42,276 --> 00:06:43,985
Here is the editorial question.

66
00:06:43,985 --> 00:06:47,404
A third of this company belonged to the Swedish taxpayer.

67
00:06:47,404 --> 00:06:55,608
The finance ministry's ownership unit sat on the nomination committee, chose the chairman and published a policy requiring state companies to be "role models".

68
00:06:55,608 --> 00:06:56,976
What did that guarantee?

69
00:06:56,976 --> 00:07:05,864
Judged by the record: a sustainability section in the annual report, a board that met, and a chairman who could be removed when the story broke.

70
00:07:05,864 --> 00:07:09,966
It did not guarantee that anyone in Stockholm asked who owned Takilant.

71
00:07:09,966 --> 00:07:13,043
State ownership was a promise about procedure, not outcome.

72
00:07:13,043 --> 00:07:22,273
When the outcome was three hundred million dollars to a president's daughter, the state's remedy was to change the directors and let foreign prosecutors do the rest.

73
00:07:22,673 --> 00:07:24,375
Telia was not alone.

74
00:07:24,375 --> 00:07:39,693
VimpelCom, Amsterdam-listed and Russian-controlled, went first, in February 2016: seven hundred and ninety-five million dollars to the United States and the Netherlands for a hundred and fourteen million in payments to Takilant, plus a compliance monitor.

75
00:07:39,693 --> 00:07:41,820
Telia followed in September 2017.

76
00:07:41,820 --> 00:07:48,203
MTS, the largest Russian operator, settled for eight hundred and fifty million in March 2019.

77
00:07:48,203 --> 00:07:56,287
Three companies, one recipient, two point six billion dollars in penalties, nearly all collected in Washington and The Hague.

78
00:07:56,287 --> 00:08:10,328
Telia's was then the third-largest foreign-bribery resolution in American history, behind Odebrecht and Siemens: three times the bribes paid, because the American method prices the profit the bribe bought, then adds a fine.

79
00:08:10,728 --> 00:08:23,638
A year after the Americans and Dutch had proposed one point four billion, on 21 September 2017 the group signed a three-year deferred prosecution agreement with the Justice Department; the Uzbek subsidiary Coscom pleaded guilty in Manhattan.

80
00:08:23,638 --> 00:08:33,756
The criminal penalty was set at five hundred and forty-eight million dollars and split evenly with the Dutch, whose jurisdiction came from three Telia holding companies in the Netherlands.

81
00:08:33,756 --> 00:08:48,061
The SEC took four hundred and fifty-seven million in disgorgement, but agreed that up to two hundred and eight and a half million could be credited if Sweden, or the Netherlands, confiscated the profits themselves, within five hundred and forty days.

82
00:08:48,061 --> 00:08:52,248
That clause is the hinge of the second half of this story.

83
00:08:52,648 --> 00:08:54,816
Follow the money to its destinations.

84
00:08:54,816 --> 00:09:00,958
The United States Treasury received about five hundred and twenty-three million dollars, once the credit is netted.

85
00:09:00,958 --> 00:09:13,605
The Dutch prosecution service received four hundred and eighty-two and a half million: its own two hundred and seventy-four, plus, in March 2019, the two hundred and eight and a half million reserved for Sweden.

86
00:09:13,605 --> 00:09:16,134
Sweden received nothing from the corporate settlement.

87
00:09:16,134 --> 00:09:20,832
Uzbekistan, whose citizens were on the wrong end of it, received nothing either.

88
00:09:20,832 --> 00:09:23,722
Sweden was in the courtroom the whole time.

89
00:09:23,722 --> 00:09:24,806
It just lost.

90
00:09:25,206 --> 00:09:36,595
The day after the settlement, Swedish prosecutors charged three men with aggravated bribery: Nyberg; Tero Kivisaari, who had run Eurasia; and Olli Tuohimaa, the lawyer who papered the deals.

91
00:09:36,595 --> 00:09:46,806
On 15 February 2019, after a trial from September 2018, Stockholm District Court acquitted all three and threw out the forfeiture claim against Telia with them.

92
00:09:46,806 --> 00:09:50,733
The reasoning was not that no money had changed hands.

93
00:09:50,733 --> 00:10:01,336
It was that Swedish bribery law, as it stood before 2012, covered only payments to public officials, and Karimova, whatever she was, held no office in telecoms.

94
00:10:01,336 --> 00:10:07,620
The prosecution had proved a payment to a president's daughter and failed to prove a bribe.

95
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:11,154
In February 2021 the Svea Court of Appeal agreed.

96
00:10:11,554 --> 00:10:17,920
The prosecutor's summary of common sense, which two Swedish courts found was not the same thing as law.

97
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:29,589
In Washington, the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act treats a president's daughter who controls access to licences as a foreign official by function; the same facts produced a guilty plea and a nine-figure penalty.

98
00:10:29,589 --> 00:10:36,662
In Stockholm, the statute of the day required a title, and the same facts produced three acquittals and no forfeiture.

99
00:10:36,662 --> 00:10:40,198
The law was amended in 2012, too late for 2007.

100
00:10:40,198 --> 00:10:48,332
So the country whose company paid the bribes could neither convict the men who approved them nor keep a krona of the proceeds.

101
00:10:48,332 --> 00:10:50,100
Not a scandal of judges.

102
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:51,515
A scandal of drafting.

103
00:10:51,915 --> 00:10:53,832
The retreat took five years.

104
00:10:53,832 --> 00:11:01,119
Nepal's Ncell went to Malaysia's Axiata for a billion dollars in 2016, the only clean profit in the exit.

105
00:11:01,119 --> 00:11:07,638
In 2018 Telia sold Azerbaijan, Georgia and, in December, its Kazakh operator Kcell to the state telecom.

106
00:11:07,638 --> 00:11:21,445
The same month it sold Ucell, the company at the centre of the affair, to the Uzbek government itself for two hundred and fifteen million dollars, a fraction of what it had paid to get in.

107
00:11:21,445 --> 00:11:23,746
Moldova, the last, went in 2020.

108
00:11:23,746 --> 00:11:35,635
In April 2016 the group dropped Sonera from its name and became Telia Company, a rebranding nobody pretended was unconnected, and moved headquarters from Stureplan to a business park in Solna.

109
00:11:35,635 --> 00:11:37,169
Fresh paint, new postcode.

110
00:11:37,569 --> 00:11:38,769
And the recipient.

111
00:11:38,769 --> 00:11:54,769
Confined to her house in 2014, secretly sentenced in 2015, jailed in 2019, and in March 2020 given a further thirteen years and four months for extortion and embezzlement, she remains in custody in Uzbekistan; her father died in 2016.

112
00:11:54,769 --> 00:11:58,769
American prosecutors indicted her in 2019 and cannot reach her.

113
00:11:58,769 --> 00:12:12,770
Her frozen fortune, estimated at up to one point four billion dollars, is trickling home: Switzerland has returned three hundred and thirteen million through a United Nations trust fund, Belgium a hundred and eight million.

114
00:12:12,770 --> 00:12:19,970
In April 2026 a Swiss court dismissed her trial because Uzbekistan would not let her leave to attend.

115
00:12:19,970 --> 00:12:23,970
The bank that held the account is still on trial.

116
00:12:24,370 --> 00:12:27,394
Telia in 2026 is a smaller, quieter company.

117
00:12:27,394 --> 00:12:36,466
Denmark was sold in 2024, the Latvian stakes are going to Riga, the television business to Schibsted, and some three thousand jobs were cut.

118
00:12:36,466 --> 00:12:41,758
Patrik Hofbauer has run it since February 2024, the fifth chief executive since Nyberg.

119
00:12:41,758 --> 00:12:50,452
Because of share buybacks, the Swedish state's holding has drifted up to around forty-one percent, larger than at any point during the scandal.

120
00:12:50,452 --> 00:12:56,879
Ask the ownership unit what changed after Uzbekistan and you will get a document about sustainable business.

121
00:12:56,879 --> 00:13:00,659
Ask what the state recovered and the answer is shorter.

122
00:13:01,059 --> 00:13:01,754
Our position.

123
00:13:01,754 --> 00:13:07,665
The Telia affair is usually told as a story about a company that lost its way abroad.

124
00:13:07,665 --> 00:13:10,447
We think it is a story about ownership.

125
00:13:10,447 --> 00:13:20,183
Sweden's stake gave the company a state chairman, a state ethics policy and, most valuable of all, the presumption that a Swedish state company does not do this.

126
00:13:20,183 --> 00:13:25,052
That presumption was extended to a deal that a twenty-minute search would have punctured.

127
00:13:25,052 --> 00:13:32,006
The exposure came from the state's own broadcaster; the penalty from foreign prosecutors; the recovery to Uzbekistan from Swiss courts.

128
00:13:32,006 --> 00:13:34,092
Sweden charged, lost, and received nothing.

129
00:13:34,092 --> 00:13:35,483
Ownership is not oversight.

130
00:13:35,483 --> 00:13:37,570
That is not a moral judgement.

131
00:13:37,570 --> 00:13:39,308
It is an accounting one.

132
00:13:39,708 --> 00:14:06,519
Every figure in this video comes, where possible, from primary documents: the US Department of Justice and SEC resolutions of 21 September 2017, the Dutch Public Prosecution Service's statements of 2017 and 2019, the Stockholm District Court judgment of February 2019 in English translation, Telia's own announcements, and reporting by SVT's Uppdrag granskning, the OCCRP, RFE/RL and Eurasianet.

133
00:14:06,519 --> 00:14:11,141
The full numbered list, with links, is in the description.

134
00:14:11,141 --> 00:14:17,150
Where a figure is approximate or contested, we have said so on screen.

135
00:14:17,550 --> 00:14:20,146
That is the Telia Uzbekistan ledger, so far.

136
00:14:20,146 --> 00:14:28,259
We will update it if the American charges against Karimova ever reach a courtroom, and as the frozen assets find their way back to Tashkent.

137
00:14:28,259 --> 00:14:40,915
If you found this useful, subscribing helps us make the next one, about a Swedish company that made its money by killing the pirates rather than paying them: Spotify, and how a Stockholm start-up took over the world's ears.

138
00:14:40,915 --> 00:14:42,862
Until then, thank you for watching.

